<div id="help" class="offcanvas ...">
  <div class="offcanvas-header">
    <h2 id="help-title">Expense policy</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="offcanvas-body">
    <p id="help-text">...</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" id="help-close" data-bs-dismiss="offcanvas">Close help</button>
  </div>
</div>

<form id="report">
  <label for="amount">Amount (EUR)</label>
  <input class="form-control mb-3" id="amount" name="amount" value="38.50">
  <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" id="submit-report">Submit report</button>
</form>

Thus natural tab order would be: open-help button, then inside offcanvas: maybe the header? Actually offcanvas is a div with tabindex="-1"? They set tabindex="-1" on the offcanvas element initially? In HTML they have tabindex="-1" attribute on #help. That means it's not focusable via tab unless script changes it. However bootstrap offcanvas likely adds tabindex="-1"? Wait they also add role dialog and aria-modal true when shown. Usually offcanvas is not focusable itself; focus is trapped inside via JS focusing first focusable element (maybe the close button?). But here they may have misconfigured focus trap causing focus to cycle only within the offcanvas (close button?) and not escape to amount.

Looking at observed focus events: Sequence:

[
 {"from":"body","sequence":0,"tag":"button","to":"#open-help"},
 {"from":"#open-help","sequence":1,"tag":"div","to":"#help"},
 {"from":"#help","sequence":2,"tag":"button","to":"#help-close"},
 {"from":"#help-close","sequence":3,"tag":"input","to":"#amount"},
 {"from":"#amount","sequence":4,"tag":"div","to":"#help"},
 {"from":"#help","sequence":5,"tag":"button","to":"#help-close"},
 {"from":"#help-close","sequence":6,"tag":"input","to":"#amount"},
 ...
]

Interesting: This suggests that pressing Tab from #help goes to #help-close (the button), then from #help-close Tab goes to #amount (good!). Then from #amount Tab goes back to #help (the offcanvas div). So the tab order is: open-help -> help (div) -> help-close -> amount -> help (div) -> help-close -> amount... So indeed after opening help, the first focus is on the help div (since it has tabindex=-1? Actually they might have focused the offcanvas element itself when shown). Then Tab goes to the first focusable element inside: help-close button. Then Tab goes to amount input. Then Tab goes back to help div (since after amount, next focusable is maybe the help div again because it's still in tab order with tabindex=-1? Actually tabindex=-1 removes from tab order; but maybe they set tabindex="0"? Not sure.)

But the issue: They need to reach #amount within 6 Tab transitions from start? Let's count: Starting from where? Step 2 is after pressing Enter on open-help. At that point, focus is presumably on #help (the offcanvas div). Then we need to Tab to #amount within 6 tabs.

Sequence: from #help (focus) press Tab -> goes to #help-close (1), Tab -> #amount (2). So that's 2 tabs, well within 6. Why did it fail? Possibly because the offcanvas div is not focusable (tabindex=-1) thus pressing Tab from #help does nothing? Actually if #help has tabindex=-1, it's not in tab order, so focus wouldn't be there. But observed focus after opening help shows focus on #help div (they have a focus event from #open-help to #help). That suggests script moved focus to the offcanvas element programmatically (maybe via .focus()). Indeed bootstrap offcanvas's _enforceFocusOnElement focuses the element when shown. That sets focus to the offcanvas div (which has tabindex="-1") but .focus() can still focus an element with tabindex=-1 programmatically. Then pressing Tab moves to next focusable element in DOM order after that element. Since the offcanvas div is not in tab order, the next focusable element after it in DOM is? Let's check DOM order: open-help button, then help div, then inside help div: header, body, p, button help-close, then after the help div closes, we have form label, input amount, button submit-report.

If the help div is not in tab order, then after focusing it programmatically, pressing Tab will go to the next focusable element in DOM that comes after the help div. That would be the help-close button? Actually the help-close button is inside the help div, which is a child; but since the help div is not in tab order, does tab traversal go into its children? Typically, if an element is not focusable, pressing Tab will skip it and go to the next focusable element in document order, ignoring its subtree? I think if an element has tabindex=-1, it's removed from tab order, but its children can still be in tab order if they have tabindex>=0. However the order is determined by the tree; if parent is skipped, you still consider its children in order? Actually tab order follows the DOM tree, but if an element is not focusable, you still traverse into its children to find focusable elements. Example: a div with tabindex=-1 containing a button; pressing Tab will go to the button if it's next in DOM after previous focusable element. So focusing the div programmatically then pressing Tab should go to the first focusable child inside it (help-close). That matches observed: from #help to #help-close.

Then from help-close, pressing Tab goes to next focusable element after the help-close in DOM: that would be after the help div closes? Actually after help-close button, the next sibling is nothing (end of offcanvas-body). Then after the offcanvas element ends, next sibling is the form. So the next focusable is the amount input. Good.

Thus from #help (focus) -> Tab to help-close (1) -> Tab to amount (2). Should succeed within 6.

Why did test fail? Perhaps because the offcanvas has attribute data-bs-backdrop="false" and data-bs-scroll="true". Also they added inline style ".offcanvas,.offcanvas-backdrop{transition:none}" but that shouldn't affect focus.

Maybe the issue is that the offcanvas has tabindex="-1" but also they set aria-modal="true" and role="dialog". According to ARIA, a modal dialog should trap focus inside it. The offcanvas is behaving as a modal (they added aria-modal=true). However they didn't implement focus trapping; they only enforce focus on the element when shown, but not trap focus inside. So when Tab leaves the offcanvas (goes to amount), focus leaves the modal, which is not desired for a modal. However the test expects that while the help panel is open (non-modal?), they want to be able to tab to amount. Actually the description says "non-modal panel is open". The offcanvas is supposed to be non-modal? They set data-bs-backdrop="false" maybe to make it non-modal. But they also set aria-modal="true" via script when shown, making it modal. That may cause conflict: they treat it as modal but want it non-modal. The test expects ability to tab out to amount while panel open (so it's non-modal). Therefore we need to prevent setting aria-modal=true and role="dialog" when the offcanvas is shown, or maybe remove those attributes.

Look at bootstrap offcanvas code: In show() method, they do:

this._element.removeAttribute('aria-hidden')
this._element.setAttribute('aria-modal', true)
this._element.setAttribute('role', 'dialog')
this._element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)

Thus they always set aria-modal and role dialog regardless of backdrop setting. For offcanvas, maybe they should not set aria-modal and role dialog if backdrop is false? In Bootstrap 5, offcanvas is considered a modal-like component but they still set role dialog? I'm not sure. However the test indicates that we need to allow tabbing out.

Thus fix: In offcanvas.js, modify the show method to not set aria-modal and role when config.backdrop is false? Actually the offcanvas has backdrop false by default in data attribute: data-bs-backdrop="false". The config will read that via Manipulator.getDataAttributes. So we can check if this._config.backdrop is false, then skip setting aria-modal and role.

Alternatively, we could remove the lines that set aria-modal and role entirely, making offcanvas non-modal (but maybe still accessible). However the spec may expect role dialog for offcanvas? The test says non-modal panel is open, so we should not make it modal.

Thus edit js/src/offcanvas.js show method: remove or conditionally set those attributes.

Also need to ensure that when hiding, we remove those attributes only if we set them. Currently hide() does:

this._element.setAttribute('aria-hidden', true)
this._element.removeAttribute('aria-modal')
this._element.removeAttribute('role')
this._element.style.visibility = 'hidden'

So hide already removes aria-modal and role. So if we conditionally set them only when backdrop true, hide will still attempt to remove them (safe). If we never set them, removeAttribute does nothing.

